ry  tight financial pinch  the bank might try to take the land  so that means no jobs yeah no jobs in a  good water year you'd see water up to  about here jacob westroz with the tulare  lake basin water storage district we're  walking through here we're certainly not  swimming through here correct correct  and we should be swimming his agency's  job is to bring state water from  northern california to 190 thousand  acres of farmland through this canal  this year it's only getting about five  percent of its contractual share from  the state not looking good this year  next year's not looking good either at  all so it could be zero potentially zero  and that means it's not looking good for  the farmers people in stratford yeah no  when the farmers have less water that's  less jobs this is the only pool in town  it was it was yeah we met up with local  teacher robert iscardo at his childhood  home which used to be the unofficial  community pool for local kids it's been  dry for 15 years the water's gone and  when the water is gone the crops are  gone when the crops are gone  we don't have jobs but he refuses to  give up these days he spends his spare  time running a non-profit raising money  to save the town but for him it's about  more than just dollars we had various  grants in the past we had a 5k run but  the big goal is we want this to be a  place that people feel proud to be part  of does it feel empty  at times yes  it does but you know you don't want to  just sit in that emptiness you want to  fill it with hope you think one day  you'll fill it up oh absolutely yeah  that's the plan it's sad that hope  floats here in stratford it might be the  only thing keeping a community alive  if this pool ever gets you know to the  point where we can fill it up again  i think that would be a reflection  on the community being back to what it  was  for cbs mornings i'm evelyn taft in  stratford california  rooting for the town of stratford there  and all those towns struggl